      "subset": "S4",
      "task_type": "cross-company",
      "question": "Based on the FY2024 filings, compare the primary strategic objectives of AEP and ED.",
      "expected_answer": "AEP's filing does not explicitly state a strategic objective in the provided excerpts; it lists risk factors. ED's filing states: 'Con Edison seeks to provide shareholder value through continued dividend growth, supported by earnings growth in regulated utilities and electric transmission projects.' Thus, ED has a clear shareholder-focused strategy, while AEP's excerpt concentrates on forward-looking statements and risks.",
      "predicted_answer": "Based on the FY2024 filings, AEP and ED have distinctly framed strategic objectives.\n\n- **AEP FY2024**: AEP's core strategy rests on three pillars: (1) reinvestment in core assets, (2) investment in growth opportunities, and (3) acquisition of new assets [E13]. This includes a five-year, $54 billion capital investment plan, adding more than 20,000 MWs of diverse generation resources through 2034, and building \"a culture of accountability and operational excellence\" while maintaining \"a strong balance sheet and achieving our financial targets\" [E13]. AEP's vision also emphasizes six core principles: delivering industry-leading customer service, providing a safe workplace, environmental respect through sustainable energy solutions, regulatory integrity, operational excellence, and strong financial discipline [E13].\n\n- **ED FY2024**: The Item 7 evidence does not contain ED's explicitly stated primary strategic objectives. The available Item 7 text discusses risk factors—specifically that \"The Companies' Strategies May Not Be Effective To Address Changes In The External Business Environment\"—and notes that \"Con Edison seeks to provide shareholder value through continued dividend growth, supported by earnings growth in regulated utilities and electric transmission projects\" [E2]. This dividend-growth framing appears in the risk disclosure rather than as a standalone strategic objectives section. Item 1 evidence (outside the requested Item 7 scope) confirms this shareholder value objective and adds that ED \"invests to provide reliable, resilient, safe and clean energy critical for its New York and New Jersey customers\" [E12], but this was not retrieved under the Item 7 scope.\n\n**Comparison**: AEP provides a detailed, forward-looking strategic framework with specific capital commitments and measurable targets in Item 7, while ED's Item 7 disclosure is risk-oriented and only implicitly references shareholder value through dividend growth as a strategic aim, without comparable granularity on strategic pillars or capital plans.\n\nSources:\n[E2] ED_2024.html — Item 7. The Companies' Strategies May Not Be Effective To Address Changes In The External Business Environment\n[E13] AEP_2024.html — Item 8. FINANCIAL STATEMENTS AND SUPPLEMENTARY DATA (Executive Overview/Company Overview)",
